# The Rocci stack

Rocci is four layers. You can stop at the one you need.

:figure[caption: "Rocci is four layers. Stop at the one you need."] {{
    :img[src: "/assets/diagrams/stack.svg", alt: "Roc and Datastar sit under Rocci templates, then Rocci standalone applications, then Rocdown interactive documents. You can stop at any layer.", loading: "lazy", decoding: "async"]
}}

1. **Roc + Datastar.** Roc owns types, helpers, SQLite, and effects.
   Datastar is the browser transport: it sends intent and morphs HTML. It
   is not a client application framework and not a Rocci language feature.
2. **Templates.** `@component`, `@css`, markup, and directives. A
   PascalCase component lowers to a pure Roc function that returns `Html`.
   Preview it without writing a server.
3. **Applications.** HTTP around those templates. Standalone and custom
   are two depths of this layer, not two products.
4. **Rocdown.** Optional Markdown-first documents and sites. Static pages
   stay Markdown; live pages reuse the application layer. This layer is
   more experimental than templates.

You do not need Rocdown to ship an app. You do not need Applications if
you only preview components into an existing `main.roc`.

## Compile to Roc

A `.rocci` file is a Roc module. Rocci recognizes a closed set of `@`
declarations. Everything else is copied into generated Roc unchanged.
Roc type-checks after lowering. Tags become `Html.*` calls. Component
tags become ordinary functions.

:figure[caption: ".rocci parses, validates, and lowers to Roc before roc compiles."] {{
    :img[src: "/assets/diagrams/compile.svg", alt: ".rocci parses template versus Roc regions, validates, and lowers to generated Roc with source-map segments. roc then type-checks and compiles that module to a native host.", loading: "lazy", decoding: "async"]
}}

Rocci does **not** type-check interpolations, params, or directive
headers. A template can parse and still fail in `roc`. The template crate
does not start HTTP or open a window; `rocci-cli` compiles the generated
module and serves it. The preview webview loads that same origin.

## Two application depths

Start with a standalone app: you declare state, a page, and a button
action; Rocci generates the server. Switch to `main.roc` when you need
routes or a platform that generator does not give you.

| Depth | What you author | What `rocci run` does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Standalone | `@context`, `@init`, `@get:view`, optional fragment / command / live routes | Generate dispatch, `/assets`, `/health`, and authored live paths |
| Custom | `main.roc` plus `.rocci` modules | Compile sibling modules and start your Roc program |

Reuse components; do not re-implement `Html`. `rocci bundle` wraps either
depth in an ad-hoc macOS `.app`.

## Deliberate limits

- Not JSX embedded throughout Roc.
- No runtime component registry and no magic `children` field.
- No markup inside arbitrary Roc expressions; use `@if` / `@for` / `@match`.
- No dynamic tag names or prop spreading.

